--- a/docs/limits.md
+++ b/docs/limits.md
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@ This page holds the detail behind the short "What is not claimed" list in the RE
   Two people sharing one address are one lead.
 * Email addresses written in capitals: the desk lower-cases an address with Python's `lower()`. Ordinary letters join (a-z and é, ü, ö, ç were checked and are safe). Some rare letters do not. Three examples, each pair is a different lead: `straße@example.com` and `STRASSE@example.com` (`ß` is the German sharp s; its capital form is `SS`), `ıstanbul@example.com` and `ISTANBUL@example.com` (`ı` is the Turkish dotless i; its capital form is the ordinary `I`), and `ſam@example.com` and `SAM@example.com` (`ſ` is the old long s, which looks like an f without the crossbar). The first-capital and mixed-case spellings (`Istanbul@example.com`, `StraSSe@example.com`) also differ from the `ı` and `ß` spellings. The same person gets two leads and two follow-up emails (in this demo, two entries in the stand-in mail system, which the tests count). Some other rare letters behave the same way (for example `µ`, `ŉ`, `ǰ`, and a Greek final sigma `ς` followed by a full stop and another letter, such as `οδος.x@example.com` and `ΟΔΟΣ.X@example.com`). A stricter way of ignoring capitals (Python's `casefold()`) would join the `ß` and `ſ` pairs but not the `ı` pair; the desk does not use it. The Kelvin sign (U+212A) and the dotted capital I (U+0130) are refused and put on the review list, not split. What to do: switch off "Hide names and email addresses" in the portal and look in the Leads list for two entries whose addresses look the same except for one rare letter: `straße@…` next to `strasse@…`, `ıstanbul@…` next to `istanbul@…`, `ſam@…` next to `sam@…` (the portal shows every address in small letters, so a capital spelling appears as the plain letters). The desk does not merge them. Tests: `tests/test_email_case_rule.py`.
 * Other address forms stay separate leads, and the desk accepts them (each is tested): a domain with non-ASCII letters and the same domain as punycode (`ada@xü.example.com` and `ada@xn--x-eha.example.com`); a comment in brackets (`ada(sales)@example.com` and `ada@example.com`); a domain in square brackets (`ada@[example.com]`). There is no 64-character limit on the part before the `@`; only the whole address is limited, to 254 characters.
+* An email address whose domain (the part after the @) starts or ends with a dot, has two dots in a row, or has a part between dots that starts or ends with a hyphen is not accepted as an address: the request goes to "Request to check" and makes no lead and no email. The desk does not check that the domain exists, how long each part is, or the part before the @ for dots in odd places. A domain needs a real dot (the ordinary full stop): a domain whose only dots are the three other full stops (U+3002, U+FF0E, U+FF61) is refused. Look-alikes of the hyphen at the edge of a part (U+FF0D, U+2010, U+2011, U+2212) and of the dot anywhere (U+2024, U+FE52) are refused; no other look-alike or Unicode folding is checked, and a part longer than 63 characters is not refused. A sender, recipient or reminder address with a second @ is refused.
 * A real mail system or CRM was not tested (except by the one probe in `docs/hubspot-probe.md`). The keyed mail system is a simulation; a real provider may remember a send key for a
   shorter or longer time. The AI step is a set of pre-written replies; nothing is said about real AI behaviour.
 * **Live model step.** The desk can ask a real AI model for a label, but only when you set `DESK_QUALIFIER=live_claude`, name a key file
